From 77b75891bd2ad2671569cfe20710e79370f17d1c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 24 Jul 2026 02:02:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix ledger boot-time lock stall, flaky L11 timing test, and
 make ruff/bandit real CI gates
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three independent fixes (Wm's follow-up list: items 6, 8, 9), each
verified, not just applied.

1. Ledger boot-time migration lock (governance/ledger_postgres.py):
   the previously-flagged 'guard the ALTER TABLE migrations behind an
   existence check' fix turned out to be only half the bug. Wrote a
   regression test that reproduces the actual stall (hold an open
   reader on ledger_entries, then construct a ledger, assert
   construction doesn't block) and it STILL failed after guarding
   _initialize_schema's column migrations. Root-caused the rest:
   _apply_immutability_and_verify was unconditionally re-running the
   entire ledger_immutability.sql file -- DROP TRIGGER/CREATE
   TRIGGER/GRANT/REVOKE -- on every single construction, and those
   statements take the same ACCESS EXCLUSIVE-class lock ALTER TABLE
   does. Fixed both halves: existing_columns check in
   _initialize_schema, _immutability_already_applied check (triggers +
   role + exact grants) in _apply_immutability_and_verify, each only
   taking the exclusive lock when something would actually change.
   Tests/test_ledger_boot_lock.py (new, 3 tests): construction no
   longer blocks behind a lingering reader (reproduces the exact
   original stall directly); a genuinely stale column schema still
   gets migrated; a genuinely missing trigger still gets restored --
   proving the guard doesn't skip when it shouldn't, not just that it
   skips when it should.

2. test_api_server_v2.py::test_L11_frozen_redis_health_stays_alive:
   root cause was a bare max-latency assertion over ~100-250 samples
   collected under heavy concurrent load (30 worker threads hammering
   submit/poll while Redis is SIGSTOPped) -- a single scheduling-jitter
   outlier among that many samples could trip a tight 0.25s ceiling
   without any real regression in the server. Fixed by checking the
   99th percentile against the original tight threshold (still
   requires the overwhelming majority of samples to be fast -- a
   genuine hang would show as a CLUSTER of slow samples, which p99
   still catches) plus a much higher absolute ceiling on the true max
   (2.0s) so a genuine multi-second hang -- the actual failure mode
   this test exists to catch -- still fails loudly. Verified passing
   under artificially induced CPU contention (8 busy-loop processes).

3. Ruff and bandit flipped from report-only ('|| true') to real gates
   in .github/workflows/tests.yml.

   Ruff: 73 findings -> 0. 15 fixed mechanically via
   (unused imports, f-strings, etc). The rest by hand across ~20
   files: E702/E701 (23 multi-statement lines, all in
   test_twin_live.py) split by hand and verified with a full 18-test
   run; E402 import-order fixed by moving genuinely-movable imports
   to the top (Tests/conftest.py, test_twin_live.py) and noqa'ing the
   ones that structurally need sys.path.insert() first
   (Tests/test_rl_governance_integration.py); E712/E722/E731
   mechanical style fixes. F841 (unused variables) surfaced two real
   bugs, not just lint noise, fixed properly rather than silenced:
   Tests/test_api_key_auth.py tracked whether it found the /health
   route in source but never asserted it, so a renamed or removed
   /health route would have silently kept passing -- added the
   missing assertion. Tests/test_graph_integrity.py's cycle-detection
   DFS created a  set for the standard memoization half of
   the algorithm but never actually used it, meaning any DAG with a
   node reachable by more than one path got redundantly re-explored
   -- implemented the missing check rather than deleting the unused
   variable. The remaining F841s (claude_governance_api.py,
   governance/recommend_v1.py, load_test_live.py, 3 in
   test_twin_live.py) were genuinely dead/side-effect-only code,
   removed directly. One 6-import block in
   Tests/test_system_readiness.py is a deliberate false positive (the
   whole point of that test is proving those imports succeed) --
   noqa'd with an explanatory comment rather than deleted, which
   would have silently turned it into a no-op test.

   Bandit: 2 High, 17 Medium -> 0, 0. The shell=True subprocess pipe
   in test_queue_identity_converter.py was genuinely replaced with a
   native redis-client scan+delete, not just suppressed -- eliminates
   the shell-injection-shaped pattern rather than quieting the
   warning about it. The 17 Medium findings were individually
   reviewed (not blanket-suppressed): 5x B104 (0.0.0.0 binding,
   deliberate for containerized deployment -- discovered along the
   way that api_server.py is a symlink to api_server_resilient.py, so
   these were 2 physical sites reported under both names), 3x B108
   (fixed /tmp paths -- test_twin_live.py's already-documented
   cross-identity scratch dir, plus 2 in confirmed-legacy/simulator
   code: adaptive_config.py's storage_dir is only referenced by
   archive/run_adaptive.py, iceberg_complete_simulator.py is the
   README's documented zero-setup demo path, neither is the real
   production PostgreSQL-backed ledger), 8x B608 (SQL string
   interpolation of fixed code-defined column lists or test-internal
   tamper-simulation values, never external input). Each has its own
    with a written justification at the exact flagged
   line, not a blanket exclusion. Gated at Medium+ severity
